1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,352
This lecture completes school Seven C Part A sections four and five.
We connect the physiology of exhaled carbon dioxide to ROSC
recognition, resuscitation decisions and family communication.

2
00:00:14,352 --> 00:00:26,496
The main textbook reading is integrated below in paired English and
Chinese. It covers the named adult-arrest section and clearly labels
older statements that differ from current local guidance.

3
00:00:26,496 --> 00:00:39,384
No carbon-dioxide number independently proves a good outcome or
authorizes stopping. Every interpretation needs the patient, signal
quality, intervention history and applicable decision pathway.

4
00:00:39,384 --> 00:00:50,952
Our aim is a complete reasoning sequence: useful observation,
justified action, reassessment and an accurate account to the team,
clinical support and family.

5
00:00:50,952 --> 00:01:02,208
Carbon dioxide is produced by metabolism and carried in blood to the
lungs. It must reach ventilated lung units and then the sampling
interface before the monitor can display it.

6
00:01:02,208 --> 00:01:15,384
During CPR, compressions can generate some pulmonary blood flow. With
ventilation and other factors reasonably stable, a change in flow may
change the amount of carbon dioxide reaching the sensor.

7
00:01:15,384 --> 00:01:26,544
This is a physiological relationship, not a direct measurement of
every organ's perfusion or metabolic viability. A low display can
arise from several links in the chain.

8
00:01:26,544 --> 00:01:35,064
Ask where the chain could have changed before deciding what the number
means. That prevents both false reassurance and premature pessimism.

9
00:01:35,064 --> 00:01:50,040
A time capnogram plots exhaled carbon-dioxide concentration or
pressure over time. The early expiration, rising phase, alveolar
plateau and inspiratory fall help explain where the end-tidal value
comes from.

10
00:01:50,040 --> 00:02:02,664
Check that a plausible repeated waveform accompanies ventilation and
that the displayed units and interface are understood. A disconnected
line or poor sample can still create a misleading number.

11
00:02:02,664 --> 00:02:14,088
Waveform shape, frequency, baseline and change can add context. They
do not substitute for observing chest movement, airway patency and the
patient's circulation.

12
00:02:14,088 --> 00:02:23,784
This school section focuses on arrest trends. Do not use a single
altered waveform shape to diagnose one disease without the rest of the
assessment.

13
00:02:23,784 --> 00:02:36,048
Continuous waveform capnography with clinical assessment is important
for confirming and monitoring a tracheal tube. It also helps identify
subsequent displacement or interruption of ventilation.

14
00:02:36,048 --> 00:02:49,992
A supraglottic airway or bag-mask interface may also provide sampled
carbon dioxide, but leaks and sampling behavior differ. A number from
one interface is not automatically equivalent evidence from another.

15
00:02:49,992 --> 00:03:05,352
When the trace changes, check the patient and visible airway system
promptly: seal, connections, tube position, secretions and sampling
obstruction. Keep necessary oxygenation and circulation support going.

16
00:03:05,352 --> 00:03:15,504
Evidence about a poor-prognosis cutoff in intubated adults must not be
transferred to a mask or SGA as though the same test had been
validated there.

17
00:03:15,504 --> 00:03:30,096
When production and circulation are stable, increased alveolar
ventilation tends to lower carbon dioxide and reduced ventilation
tends to raise it. Arrest physiology adds substantial flow and
sampling limitations.

18
00:03:30,096 --> 00:03:44,832
Therefore, do not teach that all underventilation produces a low
ETCO2. A low sample with poor chest rise may reflect very little
exhaled gas reaching the sensor rather than a simple blood-gas
relationship.

19
00:03:44,832 --> 00:03:58,704
Excessive ventilation may reduce venous return as well as wash out
carbon dioxide. Deliberately ventilating faster to improve a
saturation or reduce a number can worsen the circulation you are
trying to support.

20
00:03:58,704 --> 00:04:12,960
Use the appropriate airway, rate and volume strategy and reassess
actual ventilation. An isolated capnography target cannot justify
ignoring chest rise, air entry or hemodynamics.

21
00:04:12,960 --> 00:04:28,368
With the airway and ventilation reasonably consistent, a falling value
may prompt review of compressor fatigue, depth, recoil, position and
interruptions. Watch the technique instead of diagnosing poor CPR from
the number alone.

22
00:04:28,368 --> 00:04:40,248
Correct an observed problem, such as leaning or an exhausted
compressor, with a coordinated change that minimizes interruption.
Then reassess the waveform and the rest of the resuscitation.

23
00:04:40,248 --> 00:04:52,320
A rise after improved compressions supports a useful response but does
not prove every organ is adequately perfused. Patient factors, cause
and ventilation still influence the result.

24
00:04:52,320 --> 00:05:04,080
Do not give an extra drug, excessive breaths or untrained compressions
merely to reach a desired display. Quality monitoring should improve
actual care rather than replace it.

25
00:05:04,080 --> 00:05:16,416
During a transfer, the trace suddenly falls after being stable. Do not
decide immediately that circulation has failed or that the monitor is
wrong; both patient and equipment need prompt assessment.

26
00:05:16,416 --> 00:05:31,008
Check ventilation, airway position and seal, visible connections and
the sampling path while another clinician maintains effective CPR.
Look for a change in the patient or a newly developing cause.

27
00:05:31,008 --> 00:05:42,720
If a connection is corrected and the trace returns, reassess the
patient rather than assuming the entire problem is solved. A sampling
fault and physiological deterioration can occur together.

28
00:05:42,720 --> 00:05:54,216
Report the time, preceding action, observed problem, correction and
response. That creates a useful clinical trend instead of a collection
of unexplained numbers.

29
00:05:54,216 --> 00:06:05,064
An abrupt sustained increase can occur when spontaneous circulation
delivers more accumulated carbon dioxide to the lungs. It is an
important cue to announce to the team.

30
00:06:05,064 --> 00:06:16,872
No absolute value of thirty-five or forty proves ROSC. Ventilation,
airway changes, drugs, compressions and the arrest cause can also
affect the trend.

31
00:06:16,872 --> 00:06:29,304
Check the context, look for other signs and coordinate a brief
appropriate reassessment of circulation. Do not create repeated long
pulse checks every time one breath produces a higher value.

32
00:06:29,304 --> 00:06:41,304
If spontaneous circulation is confirmed, change to post-arrest care.
If it is not confirmed, continue or promptly resume effective arrest
care and investigate the change.

33
00:06:41,304 --> 00:06:53,904
The school highlights values below ten millimeters of mercury. Treat a
persistent low value as a reason to review the resuscitation, not as
direct proof of severe metabolic failure.

34
00:06:53,904 --> 00:07:08,400
Review compression quality and ventilation together, verify the
waveform and interface, and reconsider reversible causes. Low
pulmonary flow from an obstruction may not improve simply by telling
the compressor to push harder.

35
00:07:08,400 --> 00:07:22,104
Document what was assessed and corrected, and communicate an
unresolved low trend early. A sentence saying CPR was excellent is
less useful than actual observations and the response to correction.

36
00:07:22,104 --> 00:07:32,592
Do not stop on that number. Even a concerning prognostic association
does not replace the local eligibility, consultation and
discontinuation pathway.

37
00:07:32,592 --> 00:07:43,968
A group of patients with a particular measurement can have worse
outcomes without the measurement perfectly identifying every person
who cannot survive. Prediction and certainty are different.

38
00:07:43,968 --> 00:07:58,152
Current AHA guidance discusses a low value after prolonged intubated
adult ALS only as one part of a multimodal decision. It specifically
warns against a universal cutoff in nonintubated patients.

39
00:07:58,152 --> 00:08:10,824
A higher value is also not a guarantee that ongoing efforts will
produce a favorable neurological result. Integrate the entire clinical
course rather than using either side of a threshold as a verdict.

40
00:08:10,824 --> 00:08:21,696
For this course, the actual BCEHS pathway and appropriate clinical
direction govern the stopping decision. We do not replace them with a
textbook rhyme or foreign rule.

41
00:08:21,696 --> 00:08:34,464
In our original case, the ETCO2 rises persistently and the patient
begins normal breathing. The team announces the change and coordinates
a brief appropriate assessment that confirms circulation.

42
00:08:34,464 --> 00:08:45,672
Do not confuse an organized rhythm alone, an occasional gasp or a
pulse generated by the compressor with this confirmation. Those
findings need their own clinical interpretation.

43
00:08:45,672 --> 00:08:59,040
If the assessment does not confirm circulation, promptly return to the
indicated arrest response. A plan for ROSC must not create a prolonged
no-flow interval in a patient who remains pulseless.

44
00:08:59,040 --> 00:09:13,008
With confirmed ROSC, stop unnecessary compressions and immediately
reassess airway, breathing, perfusion and neurological state. Continue
monitoring because re-arrest remains possible.

45
00:09:13,008 --> 00:09:25,944
Global ischemia and reperfusion can injure brain, myocardium and other
organs. Restored circulation does not immediately repair the original
cause or the consequences of low flow.

46
00:09:25,944 --> 00:09:39,720
Maintain an effective airway and assess whether spontaneous breathing
is adequate. An airway device that was tolerated during arrest may
become inappropriate as consciousness and protective reflexes return.

47
00:09:39,720 --> 00:09:54,048
Monitor rhythm and perfusion, check and address glucose as indicated,
and reassess after every change. Prepare equipment and team roles for
possible re-arrest rather than dismantling the response immediately.

48
00:09:54,048 --> 00:10:07,032
Communicate the arrest course and current instability to the receiving
system. Suspected cause, ECG findings, clinical support and local
destination rules together determine the appropriate transport plan.

49
00:10:07,032 --> 00:10:20,904
During arrest and before reliable post-ROSC oxygen measurement,
avoiding hypoxemia is essential. Do not abruptly reduce oxygen because
a theoretical hyperoxia risk has become the only focus.

50
00:10:20,904 --> 00:10:34,296
Once the measurement is reliable, use the applicable local target and
titrate unnecessary excess. Saturation near its upper plateau does not
reveal how high arterial oxygen has risen.

51
00:10:34,296 --> 00:10:49,536
Know the labelled source difference: N03 essentials use ninety-two to
ninety-eight percent, whereas AHA twenty-twenty-five discusses ninety
to ninety-eight after reliable measurement. Do not invent an averaged
target.

52
00:10:49,536 --> 00:11:03,792
Consider signal quality, perfusion and the patient instead of treating
one pulse-oximeter value as infallible. Continue oxygenation
assessment and communicate uncertainty when the reading is unreliable.

53
00:11:03,792 --> 00:11:19,200
After ROSC, carbon dioxide influences cerebral blood flow. Excessively
low arterial CO2 can constrict cerebral vessels, so purposeful
hyperventilation is not a neutral way to improve a monitor number.

54
00:11:19,200 --> 00:11:31,824
End-tidal and arterial values can diverge when perfusion, ventilation-
perfusion matching and dead space change. A normal end-tidal number
does not prove a normal arterial value.

55
00:11:31,824 --> 00:11:46,800
Current N03 has an internal target difference between its essentials
and ACP paragraph. AHA's arterial range is a separately labelled
recommendation, not a way to silently resolve the local end-tidal
wording.

56
00:11:46,800 --> 00:12:01,920
Use the current applicable clinical direction, monitor ventilation and
avoid large reflex changes based on one value. Preserve these source
distinctions in your study notes rather than memorizing a fabricated
universal range.

57
00:12:01,920 --> 00:12:16,056
Avoid hypotension after ROSC and reassess actual perfusion. The
oxygen-delivery chain still requires adequate forward flow; a good-
looking saturation does not protect a poorly perfused brain.

58
00:12:16,056 --> 00:12:32,112
The local N03 text contains differing systolic wording and a mean-
pressure reference near sixty-five. AHA twenty-twenty-five uses at
least sixty-five mean arterial pressure as a minimum, with
individualized clinical context.

59
00:12:32,112 --> 00:12:44,592
The textbook's generic one-to-two-liter bolus is not a universal
order. Assess volume loss, pump function and response, and stay within
the current local fluid plan and permissions.

60
00:12:44,592 --> 00:12:54,912
Persistent shock may require advanced clinician support. Understanding
vasopressors in the reading does not give a PCP unrestricted
permission to start an infusion.

61
00:12:54,912 --> 00:13:08,688
Do not deliberately cool every survivor with large cold-fluid boluses.
Temperature management is a monitored strategy; traumatic injury and
spontaneous hypothermia add important qualifications.

62
00:13:08,688 --> 00:13:21,552
A head-up position after ROSC may be useful when compatible with
airway, perfusion and injury. This is not permission to elevate the
head during ongoing arrest as a routine CPR technique.

63
00:13:21,552 --> 00:13:36,384
N03 says about ten minutes before post-ROSC twelve-lead acquisition,
while its checklist says five to ten. AHA recommends as soon as
feasible and highlights early false-positive patterns and repeat
recordings.

64
00:13:36,384 --> 00:13:49,032
Do not combine those statements into an invented compromise clock. Use
current local direction and communicate the actual time, tracing and
clinical state without delaying urgent coordination.

65
00:13:49,032 --> 00:14:02,016
Withholding CPR concerns whether resuscitation should begin, including
obvious death, applicable lawful direction and safety. Discontinuation
concerns stopping efforts that are already underway.

66
00:14:02,016 --> 00:14:14,880
Recognition of life extinct is the required assessment following the
appropriate discontinuation process, except for the separate obvious-
death situation. It is not simply another name for the decision to
stop.

67
00:14:14,880 --> 00:14:28,536
The school introduces these decisions together, but the current local
R01, R02 and R03 assign distinct requirements. Keep the sequence and
the evidence for each decision clear.

68
00:14:28,536 --> 00:14:42,552
An isolated poor ETCO2, a family request or an elapsed timer cannot
substitute for that whole pathway. Uncertainty is a reason to seek
appropriate clinical support, not to invent criteria.

69
00:14:42,552 --> 00:14:56,712
Current obvious-death examples include decomposition, dependent
lividity or whole-body rigor, and specified injuries incompatible with
life. A cold or stiff body is not automatically the same finding.

70
00:14:56,712 --> 00:15:12,792
An applicable lawful direction may include an advance directive, MOST,
No CPR form or recognized No CPR identification. Verify relevance and
authority rather than treating every paper or relative's statement as
equivalent.

71
00:15:12,792 --> 00:15:23,976
No CPR does not mean no appropriate care or no family support. Follow
the applicable direction and care plan without turning the absence of
CPR into abandonment.

72
00:15:23,976 --> 00:15:37,824
If the criteria are unclear, start the appropriate care and seek
CliniCall support under the guidance. Scene safety remains essential;
clinical uncertainty does not require entering an unsafe scene.

73
00:15:37,824 --> 00:15:58,128
For a PCP- or EMR-led arrest without ACP or CCP present, current R02
requires early CliniCall contact after initial CPR, analysis and
indicated shocks. Initial care takes priority; the first call is not
deferred until resuscitation seems hopeless.

74
00:15:58,128 --> 00:16:11,568
A designated team member can communicate while others maintain care.
The consultation may clarify treatable causes and the plan for
continued efforts, selected conveyance or later discontinuation.

75
00:16:11,568 --> 00:16:21,624
A prior consultation may include standing direction for a specified
later point. That is different from independently deciding to stop
when a minimum duration passes.

76
00:16:21,624 --> 00:16:32,592
Communicate new findings or uncertainty that may change that
direction. Do not treat a phone call as blanket legal immunity or
permission to ignore a changed patient.

77
00:16:32,592 --> 00:16:45,024
Prepare the collapse and witness history, bystander actions, actual
resuscitation times and initial rhythm. Distinguish facts from
estimates and state when the history is uncertain.

78
00:16:45,024 --> 00:16:59,376
Summarize current physiology and care: compression quality, analyses
and shocks, airway and ventilation, capnography trend, access or
medicines actually given, and any period of ROSC.

79
00:16:59,376 --> 00:17:10,896
Report relevant causes and interventions with results, not merely that
the Hs and Ts were done. A negative or unavailable assessment is
different from a cause being excluded.

80
00:17:10,896 --> 00:17:22,416
Ask a clear question about the next plan and read back the direction,
including conditions and timing. Document who gave it, what it
authorized and what prompted later updates.

81
00:17:22,416 --> 00:17:37,128
R02 describes several rapid-discontinuation considerations, not a rule
that a first nonshockable rhythm or low ETCO2 is enough. The
prolonged-no-flow route requires all of its stated elements.

82
00:17:37,128 --> 00:17:51,072
That route includes at least twenty minutes observed unresponsive and
presumed pulseless before emergency-service arrival, no CPR during
that period, absent signs of life and the specified nonshockable
rhythm evidence.

83
00:17:51,072 --> 00:18:08,184
The medical terminal-illness consideration describes imminent
unavoidable death and unsuccessful expected CPR, not simply anyone
with cancer or a chronic condition. Lawful direction and verified
representative authority are separate considerations.

84
00:18:08,184 --> 00:18:21,024
Do not apply these as independent student decisions. Consult through
the actual pathway, preserve legal-direction distinctions and obtain
clarification whenever authority or facts are uncertain.

85
00:18:21,024 --> 00:18:32,832
The general twenty-minute minimum pathway is different from the rapid
prolonged-no-flow route. One describes a resuscitation interval; the
other includes the pre-arrival no-CPR history.

86
00:18:32,832 --> 00:18:45,480
Current R02 body requires all three: not witnessed by paramedics, EMRs
or first responders, nonshockable throughout, and no ROSC at any time
for any duration.

87
00:18:45,480 --> 00:18:55,968
Its diagram abbreviates the rhythm wording as initially nonshockable.
Do not drop the stricter throughout requirement from the body when
studying or presenting the case.

88
00:18:55,968 --> 00:19:08,592
A bystander-witnessed event is not the same as an emergency-provider-
witnessed event for that particular criterion. Document exactly who
saw what, and do not stop simply because the clock reaches twenty.

89
00:19:08,592 --> 00:19:22,728
R02 describes a thirty-minute minimum pathway for initially
nonshockable arrests and forty minutes for initially shockable
arrests. These are minimum considerations, not independent countdown-
to-death rules.

90
00:19:22,728 --> 00:19:35,904
A patient initially in VF does not become a twenty-minute-pathway
patient merely because the latest analysis shows asystole. Initial
rhythm and the entire resuscitation course remain relevant.

91
00:19:35,904 --> 00:19:49,080
Any transient ROSC must be reported; do not erase a brief circulation
period because arrest recurred. Clarify how actual timing and changed
circumstances affect the plan with clinical support.

92
00:19:49,080 --> 00:20:00,888
Continue the required treatment and assessment while using the
appropriate consultation and standing direction. Do not invent clock
resets or add drug rounds from an unrelated textbook rule.

93
00:20:00,888 --> 00:20:13,608
A duration only has meaning within the correct patient population and
clinical pathway. Hypothermia, trauma and toxicological or other
special circumstances may need a different approach.

94
00:20:13,608 --> 00:20:26,616
Confirm the actual care, rhythm course, signs of life, causes and
response. A poorly delivered interval cannot be presented as a
complete high-quality resuscitation simply because time elapsed.

95
00:20:26,616 --> 00:20:45,936
R02 contains lawful- or verified-direction exceptions, while R03 asks
for confirmation when CPR has been commenced. Preserve those source
statements and promptly clarify the actual situation rather than
inventing a universal exception or ignoring patient direction.

96
00:20:45,936 --> 00:20:59,328
Clinical consultation and accurate documentation support accountable
care. Neither a rule nor a telephone call guarantees immunity,
certainty of prognosis or permission to omit required assessment.

97
00:20:59,328 --> 00:21:15,072
Once the appropriate discontinuation process is complete, R03
specifies how life extinct is assessed. Except for obvious-death
circumstances, at least two paramedics or EMRs are involved in
independent confirmation.

98
00:21:15,072 --> 00:21:27,336
This is not simply checking that the monitor is flat or pupils are
fixed. The actual assessment specifies heart sounds, breathing
observations and a central pulse over defined intervals.

99
00:21:27,336 --> 00:21:38,472
Keep noise and movement low enough for an adequate assessment, and
record what each clinician actually found. Do not copy another
provider's findings as your own examination.

100
00:21:38,472 --> 00:21:49,176
Uncertainty means obtain further clinical direction. The generic pupil
wording in the school summary is not a substitute for the currently
served R03 procedure.

101
00:21:49,176 --> 00:22:02,928
The current R03 describes one clinician simultaneously listening for
heart and breath sounds and observing respiratory effort for ninety
seconds, while the second assesses the carotid pulse for ninety
seconds.

102
00:22:02,928 --> 00:22:14,208
These are concurrent roles in a two-provider assessment, not the
ordinary initial brief arrest-recognition check and not a reason to
delay starting indicated CPR for ninety seconds.

103
00:22:14,208 --> 00:22:28,248
Its additional asystole or very slow PEA observation is labelled ACP
and CCP, and ultrasound assistance is labelled CCP. Do not erase those
scope labels when making a PCP study card.

104
00:22:28,248 --> 00:22:39,768
Record the real assessment and times after the appropriate direction.
Environmental hypothermia is an important exception and must not be
forced through a routine ROLE checklist.

105
00:22:39,768 --> 00:22:52,632
In environmental hypothermia, absence of usual signs cannot be used
routinely to record life extinct. Seek the appropriate clinical
direction and rewarming-capable destination planning.

106
00:22:52,632 --> 00:23:05,232
Traumatic arrest prioritizes particular reversible causes and has its
own time-critical transport considerations. This adult medical-arrest
lecture does not replace the traumatic pathway.

107
00:23:05,232 --> 00:23:17,592
A prediction rule from one population must not be used as certain
evidence in another. Toxicological events and unusual circumstances
require the relevant expertise and pathway.

108
00:23:17,592 --> 00:23:32,448
If the scene history, legal direction, assessment or consultation plan
is unclear, state the uncertainty and obtain clarification. Inventing
a confident answer is not safer than communicating what is unknown.

109
00:23:32,448 --> 00:23:45,144
When staffing and circumstances permit, assign someone to explain what
is happening and support the appropriate family or support person. Do
not let all communication wait until a final decision.

110
00:23:45,144 --> 00:23:57,336
Ask about relationship, preferred name, language and cultural needs
without assuming one family pattern. Arrange suitable interpretation
rather than relying on medical jargon.

111
00:23:57,336 --> 00:24:08,088
Acknowledge useful bystander actions, such as calling for help or
attempting CPR, without guaranteeing that those actions will produce
survival or blaming them for a poor outcome.

112
00:24:08,088 --> 00:24:20,448
Keep privacy and scene safety in view. Removing PPE is appropriate
only when the clinical exposure risk permits it; a compassionate
interaction must not require unsafe removal.

113
00:24:20,448 --> 00:24:33,768
After the appropriate process establishes death, give the news plainly
and accurately. For example: I am very sorry. The treatment has not
restored circulation, and Sam has died.

114
00:24:33,768 --> 00:24:44,640
Avoid phrases that may imply sleep, temporary absence or that the
person might still be alive elsewhere. Use a calm tone and the
person's name when appropriate.

115
00:24:44,640 --> 00:24:56,952
Describe only the care actually provided. Do not say every possible
intervention was tried, promise an exact cause of death, or claim
advanced treatments that the team did not perform.

116
00:24:56,952 --> 00:25:08,280
Then pause. Remain present and listen rather than filling the silence
with a long technical explanation. Offer practical next steps under
the actual service process.

117
00:25:08,280 --> 00:25:18,192
A family member asks whether anything else can be done. Acknowledge
the shock and answer the actual question without becoming defensive or
listing your credentials.

118
00:25:18,192 --> 00:25:31,272
A truthful response may explain that the team provided the indicated
treatment, obtained clinical direction and completed the required
assessment, but circulation did not return. Match the statement to the
facts.

119
00:25:31,272 --> 00:25:45,312
Do not imply the heart must have no electrical activity if the
assessment involved PEA, or that a protocol can mathematically prove
zero chance in every individual. Clear language need not create false
claims.

120
00:25:45,312 --> 00:25:57,264
Allow further silence, answer honestly and explain what happens next
within the actual process. Respectful presence is care; it is not an
attempt to force acceptance immediately.

121
00:25:57,264 --> 00:26:10,968
Record actual times and clearly identify estimates. Include initial
rhythm, changes, bystander and team interventions, pauses, shocks,
airway findings and any transient ROSC.

122
00:26:10,968 --> 00:26:23,592
Document observed CPR quality and the reasons for clinical choices. Do
not state all reversible causes excluded when only a mnemonic
discussion occurred or an assessment was unavailable.

123
00:26:23,592 --> 00:26:37,488
Record clinical consultation and direction with its conditions, the
applicable discontinuation basis and the actual ROLE findings,
providers and times. A blank template is not evidence of an
examination.

124
00:26:37,488 --> 00:26:50,256
Describe relevant family communication and the subsequent service
steps honestly. Preserve the record and scene requirements; no
procedure should be performed after death merely to gain practice.

125
00:26:50,256 --> 00:27:05,304
In ending A, a sustained rise and the coordinated clinical assessment
confirm ROSC. The team supports airway, ventilation and perfusion,
communicates the course and plans definitive care while preparing for
re-arrest.

126
00:27:05,304 --> 00:27:19,440
In ending B, the value stays low despite reviewed technique and no
circulation is confirmed. The team continues appropriate care,
investigates causes and uses the early consultation and applicable
pathway.

127
00:27:19,440 --> 00:27:33,864
Because this original patient initially had a shockable rhythm, an
eventual asystolic analysis does not create eligibility for the
general twenty-minute pathway. Communicate the whole course, not the
last strip alone.

128
00:27:33,864 --> 00:27:47,016
Neither ending is decided by what the team hopes to see. The
transition, continued care or eventual discontinuation must follow
verified evidence, current direction and the correct sequence.

129
00:27:47,016 --> 00:28:29,688
The assigned saturation page gives several contextual numbers: 97-100
percent as its usual reference, about 95 percent as an older-adult
example, and a broader 92-100 percent practice statement. They are not
interchangeable prescriptions for a post-arrest patient, nor proof
that every person above 90 percent is safe. Compare the reliable
reading with baseline, symptoms, oxygen support and the separately
identified current N03 or other applicable pathway. The source also
confuses hypercapnia with carbon-monoxide interference: carbon dioxide
retention and carboxyhemoglobin are different problems.

130
00:28:29,688 --> 00:29:08,184
Choose a sensor designed for the patient, site and monitor: an adult
spring clip is not automatically suitable for a small infant, and a
wrap, ear or forehead sensor has its own placement and compatibility
instructions. Inspect skin, alignment, attachment and signal quality
rather than taping any probe anywhere. An inflating blood-pressure
cuff can interrupt the pulsatile signal on that limb; do not interpret
the concurrent lost or falling oximeter signal as a new diagnosis
before checking that interaction. A different site may help, but does
not guarantee accuracy in low perfusion.

131
00:29:08,184 --> 00:29:49,320
Explain all five assigned pressure factors as interacting variables:
cardiac output supplies flow; arteriolar resistance opposes it;
circulating volume influences venous return; viscosity affects
resistance; and large-artery elasticity permits expansion and recoil.
Compliance is change in volume for a change in pressure, not another
name for peripheral resistance. A stiff artery, a constricted
arteriole and thickened blood are different mechanisms. The source
alternates elasticity and rigidity in a way that can reverse the
meaning: less arterial compliance generally means more stiffness, not
improved cushioning.

132
00:29:49,320 --> 00:30:29,712
The volume paragraph describes atrial stretch and a faster-rate
response. Distinguish that atrial or Bainbridge input from the
arterial baroreflex, where increased arterial stretch usually promotes
a slowing response. One paragraph does not mean more fluid always
improves pressure or that every increased pressure produces
tachycardia. For the weekly case, repeat a reliable pulse, pressure
and clinical assessment after the actual intervention; explain whether
flow, resistance, measurement conditions or several links may have
changed. Do not select a fluid or vasodilator solely from one
simplified factor.

133
00:30:29,712 --> 00:31:19,272
The ending glossary adds legal vocabulary. Absconding is the source
term for leaving an inpatient setting without permission, not an
independent ambulance power to confine someone. False imprisonment
concerns confinement without consent or lawful authority. The source
distinguishes assault as threatened harmful contact and battery as
intentional harmful or offensive contact. This U.S.-style distinction
is not a complete Canadian offence map: Criminal Code section265
includes intentional force without consent and specified attempts or
threats. An intentional tort is a civil-wrong category, not a synonym
for every treatment error or criminal charge. Assess consent, safety
and the real applicable authority instead of deciding from a label.

134
00:31:19,272 --> 00:32:08,664
Keep other glossary concerns distinct. Fraud concerns deception; the
source uses libel for a damaging published falsehood. Neither one-line
entry is a complete legal test. Malpractice concerns professional care
failing the applicable duty or standard, not proof that a poor outcome
alone establishes wrongdoing. Confidentiality protects entrusted
information but is not a promise that relevant information can never
be shared lawfully. Duty to warn raises a protection-and-disclosure
question; it is not permission to broadcast a diagnosis or treat every
angry statement as one universal legal trigger. Record facts, protect
information and obtain appropriate guidance through the actual BC
clinical/legal-service framework.

135
00:32:08,664 --> 00:32:53,376
The first boundary page introduces forensic psychiatric nursing:
appropriately qualified assessment, evidence handling, testimony and
links to rehabilitation or social services can connect health care
with justice systems. These are different tasks, not a qualification
gained by reading the paragraph. A paramedic can accurately document
observed behaviour, injuries, statements and care while preserving
consent, dignity and the actual evidence pathway. Do not declare legal
fitness, criminal responsibility or psychiatric diagnosis from a
difficult interaction. U.S. insurance and benefits examples remain
source context, not a reason to ration emergency assessment by
presumed ability to pay.

136
00:32:53,376 --> 00:33:03,624
Explain an abrupt rise, persistent low value and sudden drop with at
least two possible mechanisms each. State what you would assess before
changing the plan.

137
00:33:03,624 --> 00:33:13,200
Demonstrate the transition after confirmed ROSC and keep arterial
versus end-tidal measurements and local versus AHA targets clearly
labelled.

138
00:33:13,200 --> 00:33:25,992
Then explain withholding, the rapid and general discontinuation
pathways, early consultation and the current concurrent ninety-second
ROLE assessment without substituting one number for the process.

139
00:33:25,992 --> 00:33:38,280
Finish by giving a concise consultation, a factual record and a clear
compassionate family explanation. That completes the five saved Seven
C teaching sections as a connected course.
